              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "The scene where Del Boy falls through the bar is widely considered one of the funniest and most iconic moments in British television history. \n\n### Episode Details\n* **Episode Title:** \"Yuppy Love\"\n* **Season/Series:** Season 6, Episode 1\n* **Original Air Date:** 8 January 1989\n* **Written by:** John Sullivan\n\n---\n\n### The Scene\nIn the episode, Del Boy (David Jason) is trying to embrace the high-flying 1980s \"yuppie\" lifestyle, complete with a trench coat, red braces, a briefcase, and a filofax. \n\nHe and Trigger (Roger Lloyd Pack) head to a trendy, upscale wine bar to try to impress two women standing across the room. Del Boy, trying to look incredibly suave, gives Trigger some advice:\n> *\"Play it nice and cool, son, nice and cool, you know what I mean?\"*\n\nJust as Del turns to lean casually against the bar, the bartender lifts the bar flap to step out. Del Boy leans back onto thin air and falls straight through the open hatch, completely disappearing from view. \n\nThe comedy is doubly elevated by **Trigger's reaction**: he doesn't see Del fall and is left standing alone, looking around in utter confusion, wondering where his friend went in the blink of an eye. Del eventually climbs back up and acts as if nothing happened, whispering to Trigger that they should leave because Trigger is *\"cramping [his] style.\"*\n\n---\n\n### Fascinating Behind-the-Scenes Trivia\n* **Real-Life Inspiration:** Writer John Sullivan got the idea for the gag when he was in a pub and watched a man nearly fall through a bar flap in the exact same manner. Fortunately, the man caught himself, but Sullivan immediately knew it would make a brilliant scene for Del Boy.\n* **It Was Only One Take:** Because of the physical demands of the stunt, David Jason did the fall in just one take. To protect the actor, a thick mattress was placed on the floor behind the bar. \n* **The Scene was \"Padding\":** Season 6 marked a shift where *Only Fools and Horses* episodes expanded from 30 minutes to 50 minutes. The first cut of \"Yuppy Love\" was about five minutes too short, so John Sullivan quickly wrote the wine bar scene to pad out the runtime. It ended up becoming the most famous scene of the entire series.\n* **Trigger's Last-Minute Inclusion:** Early rehearsal scripts for the episode suggested that Trigger wasn\u2019t originally written into the scene. However, Roger Lloyd Pack happened to be on set that day. Sullivan realized that having Trigger stand there, entirely oblivious to Del's disappearance, would make it ten times funnier\u2014a decision that turned a great physical gag into a legendary comedic masterpiece.",
